  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure overhead early this morning will slowly shift \neastward this afternoon and evening and the flow aloft will \ngradually turn more westerly. As a result, temperatures will \nstart to moderate today with highs in the upper 50s to mid 60s. \nA warming trend continues Monday through midweek with upper-\nlevel ridging building in aloft and the flow becoming more \nsouth-southwesterly. Afternoon highs Monday through Thursday \nwill be running well above average in the 60s with some valley \nlocations even making a run at the lower 70s, especially \nWednesday and Thursday. This is more typical for mid-to-late \nSeptember than the start of November. The same trend goes for \novernight low temperatures as lows go from the 30s and 40s \ntonight and Monday night into the 50s again by midweek. While a \ncouple of sprinkles can't be ruled out during the midday and \nafternoon hours Tuesday around the Finger Lakes and Lake Plain \nas a warm front lifts through the area, the next opportunity for\nmore meaningful rainfall looks to be Thursday into Friday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs mentioned above, the next chance for any appreciable rain \nlooks to be Thursday into Friday. A deep upper trough will move\ninto the Central U.S. with a surface low expected to form over \nthe Upper Midwest. The upper trough is expected to slowly push \neast, while the surface low looks to gradually pass by to our \nnorth and west, but drags a frontal boundary through the area. \nModel guidance has trended a little slower over the past 24 \nhours with regards to the arrival of showers Thursday and the \nspeed of the front, so some uncertainty remains this far out. \nMost deterministic and ensemble guidance also suggests a \nsecondary low forms near the mid-Atlantic coast come Friday. \nTemperatures look to lower a bit late week and into next \nweekend, but still remain above average for this time of year.",
